  "worked_solution": "We wish to express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-{230}^{∘})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ where ${\\theta }_{R}$ is the reference angle corresponding to the angle $\\theta =-{230}^{∘}.$\n\nTo express $\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-{230}^{∘})$ in terms of $\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R},$ we proceed as follows:\n\n- **Step 1**: Find the corresponding coterminal angle that lies in the range $[{0}^{∘},{360}^{∘}).$ We do this by adding integer multiples of ${360}^{∘}$ until we get our desired angle.\n\n$$\n-{230}^{∘}+{360}^{∘}={130}^{∘}\\,✓\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-{230}^{∘})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{130}^{∘}.\n$$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14266661/assets/233ec1bc550f8d2a536c8f86e12b3f652bd3e4f5c907c336974cb65333541680.png)\n\n- **Step 2**: Compute the reference angle. Since $\\theta ={130}^{∘}$ is in the $2$nd quadrant, the reference angle is\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{R}={180}^{∘}-{130}^{∘}={50}^{∘}.\n$$\n\n- **Step 3**: Determine the correct sign. The sine function is positive $(+)$ in the second quadrant. Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-{230}^{∘})=+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{\\theta }_{R}=+\\operatorname{sin}⁡{50}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(-{230}^{∘})=\\operatorname{sin}⁡{50}^{∘}.\n$$",
